WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.180
Sanitizers are powerful, but safe expectations matter.

2
00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:09.080
They add runtime overhead, memory overhead, and sometimes build complexity.

3
00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:14.060
They can change timing, allocation behavior, and performance characteristics.

4
00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:23.200
That is normal for instrumented development and test builds, but it means sanitizer results should be interpreted in context rather than treated like production performance data.

5
00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:25.320
Coverage is another limitation.

6
00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:32.220
Sanitizers generally report problems in paths that actually execute under the instrumented build.

7
00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:40.960
If tests never reach a parser branch, error path, platform-specific implementation, or shutdown sequence, a defect there may remain hidden.

8
00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:50.420
This is why sanitizer use should be paired with thoughtful test design, boundary tests, integration coverage, and review of code that tests do not fully exercise.

9
00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:55.980
Platform, compiler, dependency, and build-mode support can vary.

10
00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:59.840
Some third-party libraries may not be sanitizer-clean.

11
00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:06.740
Custom allocators, low-level runtime code, and mixed toolchains can require careful integration.

12
00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:14.360
Suppressions may be necessary for known external issues, but they should be narrow, documented, reviewed, and revisited.

13
00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:20.460
A broad suppression that hides real project defects weakens the trustworthiness of the workflow.

14
00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:24.820
Most importantly, sanitizers complement secure design.

15
00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:35.480
They do not replace ownership clarity, RAII, containers and views, initialization discipline, bounds-aware APIs, static analysis, or code review.

16
00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:45.100
A clean sanitizer run should encourage confidence in the tested behavior, while still leaving room for engineering judgment about untested paths and design risk.

17
00:01:45.380 --> 00:01:48.900
Set expectations with stakeholders as well as developers.

18
00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:55.880
Sanitizer work may temporarily slow builds or expose long-standing defects that were not visible before.

19
00:01:55.980 --> 00:01:57.980
That is not a failure of the tooling.

20
00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:00.400
It is useful information about risk.

21
00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:08.980
The team should decide how to prioritize findings based on severity, reachability, ownership, and the importance of the affected component.
